ng happens), there is no firn layer—bare ice is exposed. All meltwater runs straight off and is lost permanently. So, to summarize: Your documentary wasn't wrong—it just showed one step of a complex cycle. Meltwater does refreeze inside the firn, but this is a *delay* in mass loss (or a conversion of firn to ice), not a recovery of the ice that melted from the glacier's terminus. The meltwater that actually leaves the glacier as runoff is gone forever; the glacier can only catch the tiny portion that percolates within its cold upper layers before the firn warms up. In terms of the glacier's total yearly budget, the amount that refreezes this way is almost always much less than what runs off, which is why the glacier's survival still depends entirely on fresh snowfall.
