01:09:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on a really important distinction, and I can see why my earlier answers felt contradictory. Thank you for pushing back—this is exactly the nuance that makes glaciers so interesting. Your core idea—“the glacier freezes its own melted ice back to regain size”—is *partly* correct in that some meltwater does refreeze. But the reason I, and glaciologists, say the “lake freezing over” analogy is **fundamentally wrong** is because of what “regain size” actually requires. It’s not about whether refreezing happens; it’s about the **origin of the mass that makes the glacier grow.** Let’s break it down with two different scenarios, because that’s where the misunderstanding lives. ### Scenario A: The Lake Analogy (Freezing back equals net gain) Imagine a pond that loses half its water to evaporation during summer. In winter, the *same water* (liquid) freezes into ice, restoring the pond’s size. The ice is made directly from the pond’s own water. The pond’s net mass doesn’t change—it just cycles between liquid and solid. If the pond *did* lose water to a stream, it could never get that water back unless rain or a new stream refilled it. The freezing itself doesn’t add mass; it just solidifies what’s left. Now, if you thought a glacier works like that, you’d picture meltwater pooling on the surface in summer, then simply freezing solid in winter, with the glacier “regaining” the ice it lost. **That’s the idea I said was wrong.** And it is wrong, because a glacier’s mass gain does not come from refreezing its own meltwater into ice that then makes the glacier bigger. ### Scenario B: The Glacier Reality (Refreezing is internal recycling, not a net gain) Now, what actually happens: in the accumulation zone, some of the snow that fell in previous years (firn) melts at the surface. That meltwater trickles down into the cold firn and refreezes as ice lenses or superimposed ice. This refrozen water **never left the glacier system**. It was already part of the glacier’s mass as snow; it temporarily turned liquid, then resolidified deeper in the firn. The glacier didn’t “regain” anything new—it just rearranged its existing mass. That’s why scientists call it *internal accumulation*. It does not add to the glacier’s total mass; it only converts old porous firn into solid ice, a step toward becoming glacier ice, but the mass was already there. Down in the ablation zone (where bare ice melts), virtually all meltwater runs off into rivers. None of that meltwater refreezes back onto the glacier. That mass is permanently lost. The only way to replace it is through **new snowfall**—mass imported from the atmosphere. So, when you say “regain size,” you’re essentially asking: *Can the meltwater itself become the new ice that makes the glacier grow back?* The answer is no, because: - The meltwater that refreezes was already part of the glacier’s mass; it’s not adding anything. - The meltwater that runs off is gone forever. Thus, the glacier’s size never rebounds by refreezing its melt. It rebounds by getting a fresh blanket of snow that has nothing to do with that summer’s melt. ### Why your idea feels “partly right” but is actually based on a different process You’re absolutely right that *some water refreezes inside*. But that process isn’t “the glacier freezing its melted ice back to regain size”—it’s more like your body reabsorbing water from digesting food rather than excreting it. It’s conservation of mass, not growth. Growth (net gain) comes from eating new food (snowfall). If you only recycled internal water without drinking, you’d still lose weight over time as you sweat and urinate. The confusion stems from the phrase “freezes back.” In a lake, freezing back *is* the recovery of the solid state, often from the same water, and the lake returns to its original frozen extent. In a glacier, the “freezing back” you see in documentaries is just a temporary pause in the journey of water that *still belongs to the glacier’s existing mass*. It doesn’t rebuild the snout that melted away. That rebuilding requires new snow. **So to your question**: why not just say you’re partly right? Because the whole premise—that the glacier swells again in winter because melt refreezes—isn’t just incomplete; it’s a description of a fundamentally different type of system (a static water body). A glacier is a dynamic, flowing mass where the output (runoff) and input (snowfall) are separate. The internal refreezing is real, but it’s an internal process that doesn’t increase the bank balance; only new deposits do. I apologize if that sounded like I was dismissing your observation—your instinct to question it was spot on, and the documentary you saw was showing a real detail. It’s just a detail that plays a supporting role, not the main act of how glaciers survive.
