ance** of the glacier. If the water came from old ice, the glacier has lost mass unless the exact same water refreezes perfectly, which never happens in practice because it runs off. If the water came from new snow, the glacier has gained mass, and refreezing simply locks it in. This isn't just bookkeeping—it's the difference between a glacier that can sustain itself against melt (by continuously importing new snow and converting it to ice via refreezing) and a lake that simply cycles its fixed mass. The reason your original idea—that a glacier just “freezes back up” in winter like a lake—isn't just incomplete but physically wrong is because it assumes the glacier can recover its lost mass by refreezing the very meltwater that left. It can’t, because that meltwater is gone. What it can do is convert new snow into ice, giving the appearance of “freezing back up,” but only because it received fresh material. A lake doesn’t need fresh material; a glacier absolutely does. In summary: **Water is water, but mass is mass.** The physical reality is that the glacier’s mass can only increase
