01:13:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on exactly why this is such a sticky confusion: news stories, and even some well-meaning documentaries, often use loose language that makes it sound exactly like what you’ve been picturing. A headline will say “Glacier advances after two decades of retreat,” and we all naturally imagine the ice that melted at the front somehow puffing back up or re-forming there. But what “advancing” actually means, physically, reveals the whole supply chain problem once again—and it doesn’t involve the old meltwater refreezing at the snout. ### What a Glacier Advance Actually Is A glacier advances when its **terminus (the snout) moves forward.** That can happen for two main reasons: 1. **A positive mass balance years earlier:** More snow fell in the accumulation zone a few years or decades ago. That extra mass got compressed into ice and is now slowly flowing downhill like a giant conveyor belt. When it reaches the front, if it arrives faster than melting and calving remove it, the snout pushes forward. The ice at the advancing snout isn’t “regrown” from meltwater that collected there—it’s **older ice that flowed down from up-glacier**, and it originated as **snow that fell long ago.** The glacier’s advance is a delayed reaction to past snowfall, not a local refreeze of recent melt. 2. **A slowdown in melting at the snout itself:** If a few cool, cloudy summers reduce the melt rate at the terminus, the conveyor belt keeps delivering ice, so the front pushes forward even without a huge new snowfall. Again, the ice that makes the advance is ice that already existed upstream, not the same water that melted from the terminus last year. ### The News Story’s “Building Back Up” Illusion When a reporter sees a glacier’s front extending, they often say it’s “building back up from what melted.” That’s shorthand, but it’s deeply misleading. What they’re describing is the **net effect**: a scarred, retreating glacier suddenly seems to heal and push forward. It *feels* like the ice just “grew back” in the same spot. But physically, that new ice at the front is **not the original terminus ice that melted; it’s fresh ice that flowed in from the accumulation zone.** The old meltwater is long gone, in the ocean or atmosphere. The new ice is made from snow that fell years ago, sometimes centuries ago, miles uphill. It’s not a refrozen version of the ice that was lost; it’s a completely different batch of ice, transported by gravity. Think of a conveyor belt carrying boxes (ice) to a door (the terminus). If the door is wide (high melt/calving), boxes fall off and disappear. You see the belt shorten and say “it’s retreating.” Then, if the supply room upstream starts sending boxes faster (more snow years ago), the belt pushes boxes forward and the door moves outward. Did the boxes that fell off “come back”? No—new boxes arrived from upstream. The belt advanced. That’s exactly how a glacier advances. ### Even the “Regeneration” Zone Doesn’t Explain an Advance You might ask: what about the superimposed ice I mentioned earlier? Could that cause an advance? Rarely, and only in very small, cold glaciers where virtually all accumulation both melts and refreezes on the spot, turning into a new ice layer each year. In that case, the glacier’s mass is being retained locally, but the ice that builds up is still made from that year’s snowfall, not from old ice that melted. And it’s still not the same water that ran off in previous years—it’s new snow. The glacier can thicken in its accumulation zone and eventually push ice downstream, leading to an advance at the snout years later. But again, the physical material is fresh snow, not recycled melt. ### The Persistent Myth and Why It’s So Hard to Shake News stories often say a glacier “has started to re-grow” or “is advancing again after losing X meters of ice.” The human brain, understandably, pictures the meltwater refreezing and the glacier just swelling up from the bottom like a spon