ey're describing the local regrowth of an icy surface from the melt of the current year's snow. It's a descriptive term for a transformation, not a statement about the source of the glacier's total mass. The ice in that surface layer is new ice made from new snow, not old ice reborn. I'll admit, it's an unfortunate word choice when we try to explain how glaciers work to the public. It practically invites the misinterpretation you've been pointing out all along. You weren't wrong to be suspicious — you were just uncovering a case where technical jargon and everyday language collide.
